WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
Environments: development, preview, production.

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:12.483
Most teams run at least three environments: development on a laptop, preview or staging deployments for each branch or pull request, and production.

3
00:00:12.763 --> 00:00:19.995
Vercel, for example, scopes each environment variable to Production, Preview, Development, or custom environments.

4
00:00:20.275 --> 00:00:24.925
For an LLM service, separating them is about more than databases.

5
00:00:25.205 --> 00:00:26.656
Separate keys and spend.

6
00:00:26.936 --> 00:00:32.248
Give each environment its own API key, ideally in its own Anthropic workspace.

7
00:00:32.528 --> 00:00:44.368
The rate-limits docs describe per-workspace spend and rate limits below the organization’s, so a runaway preview deployment or a load test can’t consume production’s rate limit or budget.

8
00:00:44.648 --> 00:00:51.261
A preview build should never hold the production key: preview deployments often run code that hasn’t been reviewed yet.

9
00:00:51.541 --> 00:00:54.997
Model ids are config, and changing them is a deploy.

10
00:00:55.277 --> 00:01:10.381
Pin exact model ids per environment in reviewed configuration, keep an allowlist of models you’ve evaluated, and promote a model change the way you promote code: run the Studio 3 eval and regression gate in preview, then ship.

11
00:01:10.661 --> 00:01:17.595
A model swap that arrives through an unreviewed environment-variable edit is a behavior change nobody tested.

12
00:01:17.875 --> 00:01:19.688
Same image, different config.

13
00:01:19.968 --> 00:01:25.835
Build once and promote the same artifact from preview to production, changing only configuration.

14
00:01:26.115 --> 00:01:29.123
That is what makes “it worked in staging” mean something.

15
00:01:29.403 --> 00:01:31.173
Know which environment answered.

16
00:01:31.453 --> 00:01:43.144
Log the environment, model id, and deploy version with every request (week 3 builds this into traces), so an incident review can tell a preview experiment from production traffic.
